# Port-stable camera identity: /dev/mongla_cam_forward and _downward.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `cameras.yaml` has always named the cameras by
# `/dev/v4l/by-path/platform-xhci-hcd.N-usb-...`, and those ID_PATH values are
# CORRECT -- udevadm reports exactly them. But Raspberry Pi OS's udev rules
# create only `/dev/v4l/by-id/` for USB video, never `/dev/v4l/by-path/`. So on
# this Pi both profiles named a symlink the distro does not make, both fell
# through to the integer index, and BOTH CAMERAS RESOLVED TO /dev/video0 --
# whichever node started first won and the other died with "failed to open".
# That is worse than the swap the by-path convention exists to prevent.
#
# WHY NOT by-id. The vehicle carries TWO IDENTICAL Blue Robotics cameras and
# the Sonix module already here reports `SN0001` -- a serial the vendor did not
# bother to make unique. by-id cannot tell two of them apart; the USB PORT can,
# which is the whole point of by-path.
#
# ID_PATH is the physical port, so re-plugging into the SAME socket keeps the
# name and moving to another socket changes it. That is the intended behaviour:
# a camera in the wrong port should be an error, not a silent swap.
#
# index0 only: a UVC camera exposes a second /dev/video* node for metadata that
# cannot capture, and opening it looks exactly like a dead camera.
#
#   sudo cp tools/udev/99-mongla-cameras.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=video4linux
#   ls -l /dev/mongla_cam_*
#
# Re-derive the ports for a new build with:
#   udevadm info -n /dev/videoN | grep ID_PATH=

SUBSYSTEM!="video4linux", GOTO="mongla_cam_end"
ATTR{index}!="0", GOTO="mongla_cam_end"

# ⛔ BOTH LINES WERE WRONG, and they failed in two different ways.
#
# MEASURED on the vehicle 2026-09-07 (`udevadm info -n /dev/videoN`):
#
#   Sonix "USB_2.0_Camera"    ID_PATH=platform-xhci-hcd.1-usb-0:1:1.0
#   Fantech_Luminous_C30      ID_PATH=platform-xhci-hcd.0-usb-0:1:1.0
#
# 1. The old `forward` line named `hcd.1-usb-0:2:1.0`, which MATCHES NO
#    DEVICE. So udev never created that symlink and the one on the box was
#    hand-made and stale -- it survived only because nothing checked.
# 2. The old `downward` line matched the Fantech, but the FANTECH IS THE
#    FORWARD CAMERA. The global-shutter Sonix is the BOTTOM one.
#
# So the two cameras were SWAPPED IN SOFTWARE: `pi_forward` opened the bottom
# camera and `pi_downward` opened the forward one, while every node, launch
# file and mission looked entirely correct.
#
# That is not a cosmetic swap. The bottom camera is the optical-flow velocity
# sensor (our DVL substitute). A GLOBAL shutter is what makes that work -- a
# rolling shutter skews the frame while the hull moves and corrupts the very
# displacement flow is measuring -- and the Sonix is also the only one of the
# two that exceeds 30 Hz. Measured: Sonix MJPG 640x360 210.17 Hz, Fantech a
# flat 15.00 Hz in EVERY format and resolution.
#
# MATCH ON THE MODEL, NOT THE PORT, WHILE THE TWO UNITS ARE DIFFERENT MODELS.
# The by-port scheme below is the right answer only for two IDENTICAL cameras
# (the vehicle's future pair of Blue Robotics units, whose serials are not
# unique). Today the models differ, so the model is both more specific and
# port-independent: moving a camera to another socket keeps it correct instead
# of silently renaming it.
ENV{ID_MODEL}=="Fantech_Luminous_C30", SYMLINK+="mongla_cam_forward"
ENV{ID_MODEL}=="USB_2.0_Camera",       SYMLINK+="mongla_cam_downward"

# When the two identical Blue Robotics cameras land, the model stops
# discriminating and these take over. Re-derive each ID_PATH on the real build
# (`udevadm info -n /dev/videoN | grep ID_PATH=`) -- do NOT copy these values,
# one of them was wrong for exactly that reason.
#ENV{ID_PATH}=="platform-xhci-hcd.0-usb-0:1:1.0", SYMLINK+="mongla_cam_forward"
#ENV{ID_PATH}=="platform-xhci-hcd.1-usb-0:1:1.0", SYMLINK+="mongla_cam_downward"

LABEL="mongla_cam_end"
